its own fx/fy/fz by summing over all j in the same order as sequential, so that is fine because each i is independent. However, if we use double buffering with alternating arrays, we must ensure that the force computation for body i reads from the source buffer and writes to destination buffer, but the accumulation order per i remains the same (j increasing). That should be okay.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: In C's implementation, it uses `src` and `dst` buffers. For each iteration, it computes forces using src positions/velocities, then updates dst velocities and positions. The sequential code does in-place update: first compute force based on current positions (which are the same for all bodies), then update velocity and position. In C's double buffering, it reads from src (the previous state) and writes to dst (new state). That is equivalent because at iteration iter, src contains the state after iter-1 iterations, which matches sequential. So that should be correct.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that the force computation uses the same positions for all j as in sequential. In C's code, it reads `src[j].x` and `src[i].x`. That is fine because src is not modified during the iteration (only dst is written). But note: in the original sequential code, after computing forces for body i, it updates p[i] immediately? Actually no: The original loop computes all forces first (for each i) then updates positions/velocities. Wait, look at original `body_force`: It loops over i, computes fx/fy/fz by summing over j using current positions of all bodies (which are unchanged during the force computation), then updates p[i].vx etc. So it modifies velocities in place as it goes. But that means when computing forces for later i's, the positions have not changed yet (only velocities updated). However, the sequential code does: `body_force(bodies, DT, nbodies);` which loops over all i and updates velocities; then after body_force returns, it updates positions in a separate loop. So during