es contiguous chunks? Actually static schedule divides iterations among threads in blocks; that's similar to manual partitioning but done by OpenMP). But the requirement says \"Manual OpenMP thread partitioning instead of worksharing loop\" - we should implement it manually using omp_get_thread_num and stride, as C did. That might be slower though.\n\nGiven the history, the best was 0.15983s with SoA + unrolled + single parallel region static schedule (no double buffering). The current task wants to integrate all three sources. We need to produce a candidate that includes double buffering and manual partitioning from C, plus SoA and unrolling from A/B. But we must ensure it compiles and is correct.\n\nLet's design the code:\n\n- Read input into Body array (as original) or directly into SoA arrays? Source A used SoA: separate float arrays px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz. We'll adopt that.\n- Allocate two sets of SoA buffers for double buffering: e.g., px_src, py_src,... and px_dst, ... but we can alternate by swapping pointers each iteration. Since N_ITER=10 (even), final state will be in the buffer used at even iterations? Actually if we start with src = initial, dst = other, after 1 iter swap, etc. After 10 iters, since 10 is even, the final state is in the original src? Let's think: iteration 0 uses src=initial, writes to dst; then swap so next iteration src=dst (which now has new state), and dst becomes old initial buffer (now free). So after each iteration we swap. After N_ITER iterations, if N_ITER even, final is in the buffer that was used as dst on last iteration? Actually let's simulate: start with A=initial, B=unused. iter0: src=A, dst=B -> write to B; then swap pointers so now src=B, dst=A. iter1: src=B (new state), dst=A -> write to A; swap again. So after each odd iteration we are back to writing into the other buffer. After 10 iterations (even), we have swapped 10 times? Actually we swap at end of each iteration. After iter0, we swap so src becomes B. After iter1, swap so src becomes A. So after even number of iterations, src is A again? Let's count: start with src=A, dst=B. After iter0 (i=0), write to B, then swap -> src=B, dst=A. After iter1, write to A, swap -> src=A, dst=B. So after 2 iters, back to original. Since N_ITER=10 even, after 10 iters we have swapped 10 times, so final state is in the buffer that was written on last iteration (iter9). Iter9: since iter index starts at 0, for i=9 (odd), src? Let's see pattern: For even i (0,2,4,...) we write to dst which initially B then A alternating. Actually after each swap, the roles alternate. It might be easier to just keep track with a pointer and use parity.\n\nAlternatively, we can avoid double buffering entirely because it doesn't change correctness if we do in-place updates as long as we compute all forces before updating positions? But original does force computation then position update separately; that is effectively two passes per iteration. In C's double buffering, they combine force and position update into one loop over i (since each thread handles a subset of i). That might be more efficient because it avoids reading/writing the same array multiple times? Actually in sequential, body_force updates velocities for all bodies, then separate loop updates positions. With SoA, we could do: compute forces for all i and update vx[i] etc., then after that loop, update px[i] += vx[i]*DT. That's two loops per iteration. C combined them into one loop over i (each thread does force computation and position update for its assigned bodies). That reduces memory traffic because you don't need to read velocities again? But it requires double buffering because if you update positions in place while other threads are reading them, there would be races. So double buffering allows combining the two steps into one loop.\n\nSo we can adopt C's approach: for each iteration, each thread processes a subset of i (manual partitioning), computes forces using src arrays, then writes dst velocities and positions. Then barrier to ensure all threads have written before next iteration swaps buffers.\n\nWe also need unrolled inner loop from B. So inside the force computation for body i, we use unrolled j-loop by 4 with independent sqrt/div computations, preserving order.\n\nAlso source A used OpenMP static scheduling over outer loop; but C uses manual partitioning. We'll implement manual partitioning as per C: `for (int i = tid; i < nbodies; i += nthreads)`. That is a strided partition, not contiguous blocks. It might have better load balance? But it may cause more cache misses because each thread accesses non-contiguous bodies. However, with SoA, the arrays are separate per coordinate, so accessing px[i] for i = tid, tid+nthreads,... means each thread touches every nth element of each array; that could be bad for cache locality. C used AoS (Body struct) and manual partitioning; but we have SoA. Maybe better to use contiguous chunks: `int start = tid * nbodies / nthreads; int end = (tid+1)*nbodies/nthreads;` That would give each thread a contiguous block of bodies, which is cache-friendly for SoA because each array is accessed sequentially within the block. But C explicitly used strided partitioning. The task says \"Manual OpenMP thread partitioning instead of worksharing loop\" - it doesn't specify stride vs block. We can choose either as long as we do manual partitioning. To be safe and likely faster, use contiguous blocks.\n\nWe also need explicit barrier after each iteration (as C did). That is necessary because next iteration reads from the buffer that was just written by all threads.\n\nNow about aligned SIMD: B used `#pragma omp for simd aligned(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz:16)` but we saw that syntax failed. We can use `#pragma omp simd` inside a loop? Or maybe we don't need it; the compiler with -O3 might auto-vectorize. The previous successful attempt 4acd2d74c5d74746 did not include aligned clause and still got good performance. So we can omit that to avoid compilation errors.\n\nWe must ensure includes: <omp.h> for omp_get_thread_num etc.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <omp.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10\n#define DT 0.01f\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int nbodies;\n    if (fread(&nbodies, sizeof(nbodies), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    size_t count = (size_t)nbodies * 6;\n    float *tmp = (float *)malloc(count * sizeof(float));\n    if (!tmp) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    if (fread(tmp, count * sizeof(float), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n\n    // Allocate two sets of SoA buffers for double buffering\n    float *px[2], *py[2], *pz[2], *vx[2], *vy[2], *vz[2];\n    for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {\n        px[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        py[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        pz[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vx[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vy[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vz[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        if (!px[b] || !py[b] || !pz[b] || !vx[b] || !vy[b] || !vz[b]) {\n            perror(\"malloc\");\n            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Initialize buffer 0 with input data\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        px[0][i] = tmp[6*i];\n        py[0][i] = tmp[6*i+1];\n        pz[0][i] = tmp[6*i+2];\n        vx[0][i] = tmp[6*i+3];\n        vy[0][i] = tmp[6*i+4];\n        vz[0][i] = tmp[6*i+5];\n    }\n    free(tmp);\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        int nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();\n\n        for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n            // Determine source and destination buffers based on iteration parity\n            int src_idx = iter % 2;\n            int dst_idx = 1 - src_idx;\n\n            float *src_px = px[src_idx], *src_py = py[src_idx], *src_pz = pz[src_idx];\n            float *src_vx = vx[src_idx], *src_vy = vy[src_idx], *src_vz = vz[src_idx];\n            float *dst_px = px[dst_idx], *dst_py = py[dst_idx], *dst_pz = pz[dst_idx];\n            float *dst_vx = vx[dst_idx], *dst_vy = vy[dst_idx], *dst_vz = vz[dst_idx];\n\n            // Manual partitioning: contiguous blocks\n            int start = (int)(((long long)tid * nbodies) / nthreads);\n            int end   = (int)(((long long)(tid+1) * nbodies) / nthreads);\n\n            for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n                float fx = 0.0f, fy = 0.0f, fz = 0.0f;\n                const float xi = src_px[i], yi = src_py[i], zi = src_pz[i];\n\n                int j = 0;\n                // Unroll by 4 to expose independent sqrt/div computations\n                for (; j + 3 < nbodies; j += 4) {\n                    float dx0 = src_px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy0 = src_py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz0 = src_pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist0 = dx0*dx0 + dy0*dy0 + dz0*dz0 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist0 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist0);\n                    float inv_dist3_0 = inv_dist0 * inv_dist0 * inv_dist0;\n\n                    float dx1 = src_px[j+1] - xi;\n                    float dy1 = src_py[j+1] - yi;\n                    float dz1 = src_pz[j+1] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist1 = dx1*dx1 + dy1*dy1 + dz1*dz1 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist1 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist1);\n                    float inv_dist3_1 = inv_dist1 * inv_dist1 * inv_dist1;\n\n                    float dx2 = src_px[j+2] - xi;\n                    float dy2 = src_py[j+2] - yi;\n                    float dz2 = src_pz[j+2] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist2 = dx2*dx2 + dy2*dy2 + dz2*dz2 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist2 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist2);\n                    float inv_dist3_2 = inv_dist2 * inv_dist2 * inv_dist2;\n\n                    float dx3 = src_px[j+3] - xi;\n                    float dy3 = src_py[j+3] - yi;\n                    float dz3 = src_pz[j+3] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist3 = dx3*dx3 + dy3*dy3 + dz3*dz3 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist3_3 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3);\n                    float inv_dist3_4 = inv_dist3 * inv_dist3 * inv_dist3; // careful: variable name conflict\n\n                    // Accumulate in strict j order\n                    fx += dx0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fy += dy0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fz += dz0 * inv_dist3_0;\n\n                    fx += dx1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fy += dy1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fz += dz1 * inv_dist3_1;\n\n                    fx += dx2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fy += dy2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fz += dz2 * inv_dist3_2;\n\n                    fx += dx3 * inv_dist3_4; // use correct variable\n                    fy += dy3 * inv_dist3_4;\n                    fz += dz3 * inv_dist3_4;\n                }\n                for (; j < nbodies; ++j) {\n                    float dx = src_px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy = src_py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz = src_pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n                    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n                    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n                    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n                    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n                }\n\n                dst_vx[i] = src_vx[i] + DT*fx;\n                dst_vy[i] = src_vy[i] + DT*fy;\n                dst_vz[i] = src_vz[i] + DT*fz;\n                dst_px[i] = src_px[i] + dst_vx[i] * DT;\n                dst_py[i] = src_py[i] + dst_vy[i] * DT;\n                dst_pz[i] = src_pz[i] + dst_vz[i] * DT;\n            }\n\n            // Ensure all threads have written dst before next iteration reads it as src\n            #pragma omp barrier\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Final state is in buffer 0 if N_ITER even, else buffer 1 (since we start with src=0)\n    int final_idx = (N_ITER % 2 == 0) ? 0 : 1;\n\n    // Write output as Body structs (AoS) to match expected binary format\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        float out[6] = {px[final_idx][i], py[final_idx][i], pz[final_idx][i],\n                        vx[final_idx][i], vy[final_idx][i], vz[final_idx][i]};\n        fwrite(out, sizeof(float), 6, stdout);\n    }\n\n    // Free buffers\n    for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {\n        free(px[b]); free(py[b]); free(pz[b]);\n        free(vx[b]); free(vy[b]); free(vz[b]);\n    }\n    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n```\n\nBut wait: The output format is a binary file with the same structure as input: first an int nbodies? Actually original read_dataset reads an int then bodies. But write_dataset writes only the Body array, not the count. Let's check original code: `write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies)` does `fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);` So it writes just the body data, no count. The input includes an int count first. So output should be only the bodies array (6 floats per body). Our code above writes each body as 6 floats sequentially; that is equivalent to writing a Body struct because Body has exactly 6 floats in order x,y,z,vx,vy,vz. However, there might be padding? In C, a struct of 6 floats typically has no padding (size = 24 bytes). So it's fine.\n\nBut we need to ensure the output is byte-identical to sequential. The sequential writes `bodies` array as one block. Our loop writes each body individually; that should produce same bytes if there is no padding and order matches. But to be safe, we could allocate a Body array and fill it then write once. That would also avoid potential issues with fwrite buffering? Actually writing in chunks might still be fine. However, the judge compares exact output bytes; as long as we write exactly 6 floats per body in same order, it's identical.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: The original code reads input into Body array using `fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin)`. That assumes no padding. Our reading uses tmp array of size nbodies*6 and then copies to SoA. That should be fine.\n\nNow check correctness: We must ensure that the force computation for each i sums over all j in increasing order exactly as sequential. In our unrolled loop, we process j=0..nbodies-1 in groups of 4, but within each group we accumulate dx0*inv_dist3_0 then dx1... etc., which is same order. The tail loop handles remaining j. So the sum order is identical to sequential (which does j from 0 to n-1). However, note that in sequential body_force, it uses `p[j].x - p[i].x` and so on; we use src_px[j] - xi where xi = src_px[i]. That's same.\n\nBut there is a potential issue: In the original code, after computing forces for all i (in body_force), it updates velocities in place. Then later positions are updated using new velocities. Our double buffering does both velocity and position update per i within the same loop. Is that equivalent? Let's verify with an example.\n\nSequential iteration:\n- For each i: compute fx,fy,fz based on current positions (which are unchanged during this phase). Then set vx[i] = old_vx[i] + dt*fx, etc.\n- After all i done, for each i: x[i] += vx[i]*dt.\n\nOur double buffering:\n- For each i in parallel: compute fx,fy,fz based on src positions (which are the same as current positions at start of iteration). Then set dst_vx[i] = src_vx[i] + dt*fx, etc. Then set dst_px[i] = src_px[i] + dst_vx[i]*dt.\n\nThis is exactly the same: velocity update uses old velocity and force; position update uses old position and new velocity. So it's correct.\n\nNow about manual partitioning with contiguous blocks: Each thread processes a subset of i, but all threads read from src arrays (which are not modified during this iteration) so no race on reads. They write to dst arrays at distinct indices (since each i is handled by exactly one thread), so no race on writes. Then barrier ensures all writes complete before next iteration swaps buffers.\n\nOne concern: The unrolled loop uses variable names like `inv_dist3` for the fourth group, but we have a conflict with the tail loop's `inv_dist3`. In our code above I mistakenly used `inv_dist3_4 = inv_dist3 * inv_dist3 * inv_dist3;` where `inv_dist3` is actually the inverse distance of dx3? Actually in B's original unrolled code, they had:\n\n```\nfloat inv_dist3 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3);\nfloat inv_dist3_3 = inv_dist3 * inv_dist3 * inv_dist3;\n```\n\nSo they used `inv_dist3` as the inverse distance for dx3, then computed `inv_dist3_3`. In our code we should do similarly: define `float inv_dist3_local = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3); float inv_dist3_3 = inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local;` to avoid conflict with the tail loop's variable name (which is in a different scope anyway). Actually we can just use distinct names.\n\nLet me rewrite the unrolled part cleanly:\n\n```c\nfloat dx0 = src_px[j] - xi;\n...\nfloat sqrd_dist0 = ...;\nfloat inv_dist0 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist0);\nfloat inv_dist3_0 = inv_dist0 * inv_dist0 * inv_dist0;\n\n...\n\nfloat dx3 = src_px[j+3] - xi;\n...\nfloat sqrd_dist3 = ...;\nfloat inv_dist3_local = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3);\nfloat inv_dist3_3 = inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local;\n\nfx += dx0 * inv_dist3_0; ...\nfx += dx3 * inv_dist3_3; ...\n```\n\nThat's fine.\n\nAlso, we need to include <omp.h> and compile with -fopenmp. The Makefile likely already does that? We don't have control over Makefile per instructions (we can only edit src/nbody.c). But the build system probably compiles with OpenMP flags if needed. In previous experiments, they used `#pragma omp` so it must be enabled.\n\nNow about performance: This combines double buffering and manual partitioning which might not be as fast as the best previous attempt (0.1598s) because manual partitioning may have overhead. But we are required to integrate all three sources. We'll propose this candidate.\n\nWe need to ensure no compilation errors. The `#pragma omp barrier` inside a parallel region is fine. We must also handle case where nbodies might be 0? Probably not, but safe.\n\nAlso note: In the original code, there's an unused parameter argc/argv; we can ignore.\n\nNow let's produce final JSON with file content.\n\nWe need to include sources_kept as all three IDs since we are integrating them. transformations list should describe what we did.\n\nLet me write the full source carefully.\n\nI'll also consider using `restrict` qualifiers? Not necessary but could help compiler. We can add restrict to pointers in function signatures if we had functions, but here everything is in main. We can declare local pointers as restrict? In C, restrict on automatic variables is allowed? Actually restrict only applies to pointer types; you can use it on local pointer declarations: `float *restrict src_px = ...`. That might help optimization. But not required.\n\nLet's produce the code with proper formatting and comments.\n\nI'll also ensure we free all buffers at end.\n\nOne more thing: The output writing loop uses fwrite per body; that could be slower but negligible compared to computation. We can instead allocate a Body array and fill it then write once, but that would require converting SoA back to AoS. That's fine. Let's do that for clarity:\n\n```c\nBody *out = (Body *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(Body));\nfor (int i=0;i<nbodies;++i) {\n    out[i].x = px[final_idx][i];\n    ...\n}\nfwrite(out, nbodies*sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\nfree(out);\n```\n\nBut we need to define Body struct. We can keep the typedef from original.\n\nLet's include that.\n\nNow final code:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <omp.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int nbodies;\n    if (fread(&nbodies, sizeof(nbodies), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    size_t count = (size_t)nbodies * 6;\n    float *tmp = (float *)malloc(count * sizeof(float));\n    if (!tmp) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    if (fread(tmp, count * sizeof(float), 1, stdin) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n\n    // Allocate two sets of SoA buffers for double buffering\n    float *px[2], *py[2], *pz[2], *vx[2], *vy[2], *vz[2];\n    for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {\n        px[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        py[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        pz[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vx[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vy[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        vz[b] = (float *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(float));\n        if (!px[b] || !py[b] || !pz[b] || !vx[b] || !vy[b] || !vz[b]) {\n            perror(\"malloc\");\n            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Initialize buffer 0 with input data\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        px[0][i] = tmp[6*i];\n        py[0][i] = tmp[6*i+1];\n        pz[0][i] = tmp[6*i+2];\n        vx[0][i] = tmp[6*i+3];\n        vy[0][i] = tmp[6*i+4];\n        vz[0][i] = tmp[6*i+5];\n    }\n    free(tmp);\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        int nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();\n\n        for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n            // Determine source and destination buffers based on iteration parity\n            int src_idx = iter % 2;\n            int dst_idx = 1 - src_idx;\n\n            float *src_px = px[src_idx], *src_py = py[src_idx], *src_pz = pz[src_idx];\n            float *src_vx = vx[src_idx], *src_vy = vy[src_idx], *src_vz = vz[src_idx];\n            float *dst_px = px[dst_idx], *dst_py = py[dst_idx], *dst_pz = pz[dst_idx];\n            float *dst_vx = vx[dst_idx], *dst_vy = vy[dst_idx], *dst_vz = vz[dst_idx];\n\n            // Manual partitioning: contiguous blocks\n            int start = (int)(((long long)tid * nbodies) / nthreads);\n            int end   = (int)(((long long)(tid+1) * nbodies) / nthreads);\n\n            for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n                float fx = 0.0f, fy = 0.0f, fz = 0.0f;\n                const float xi = src_px[i], yi = src_py[i], zi = src_pz[i];\n\n                int j = 0;\n                // Unroll by 4 to expose independent sqrt/div computations\n                for (; j + 3 < nbodies; j += 4) {\n                    float dx0 = src_px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy0 = src_py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz0 = src_pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist0 = dx0*dx0 + dy0*dy0 + dz0*dz0 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist0 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist0);\n                    float inv_dist3_0 = inv_dist0 * inv_dist0 * inv_dist0;\n\n                    float dx1 = src_px[j+1] - xi;\n                    float dy1 = src_py[j+1] - yi;\n                    float dz1 = src_pz[j+1] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist1 = dx1*dx1 + dy1*dy1 + dz1*dz1 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist1 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist1);\n                    float inv_dist3_1 = inv_dist1 * inv_dist1 * inv_dist1;\n\n                    float dx2 = src_px[j+2] - xi;\n                    float dy2 = src_py[j+2] - yi;\n                    float dz2 = src_pz[j+2] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist2 = dx2*dx2 + dy2*dy2 + dz2*dz2 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist2 = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist2);\n                    float inv_dist3_2 = inv_dist2 * inv_dist2 * inv_dist2;\n\n                    float dx3 = src_px[j+3] - xi;\n                    float dy3 = src_py[j+3] - yi;\n                    float dz3 = src_pz[j+3] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist3 = dx3*dx3 + dy3*dy3 + dz3*dz3 + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist3_local = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist3);\n                    float inv_dist3_3 = inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local * inv_dist3_local;\n\n                    // Accumulate in strict j order\n                    fx += dx0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fy += dy0 * inv_dist3_0;\n                    fz += dz0 * inv_dist3_0;\n\n                    fx += dx1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fy += dy1 * inv_dist3_1;\n                    fz += dz1 * inv_dist3_1;\n\n                    fx += dx2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fy += dy2 * inv_dist3_2;\n                    fz += dz2 * inv_dist3_2;\n\n                    fx += dx3 * inv_dist3_3;\n                    fy += dy3 * inv_dist3_3;\n                    fz += dz3 * inv_dist3_3;\n                }\n                for (; j < nbodies; ++j) {\n                    float dx = src_px[j] - xi;\n                    float dy = src_py[j] - yi;\n                    float dz = src_pz[j] - zi;\n                    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n                    float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n                    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n                    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n                    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n                    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n                }\n\n                dst_vx[i] = src_vx[i] + DT*fx;\n                dst_vy[i] = src_vy[i] + DT*fy;\n                dst_vz[i] = src_vz[i] + DT*fz;\n                dst_px[i] = src_px[i] + dst_vx[i] * DT;\n                dst_py[i] = src_py[i] + dst_vy[i] * DT;\n                dst_pz[i] = src_pz[i] + dst_vz[i] * DT;\n            }\n\n            // Ensure all threads have written dst before next iteration reads it as src\n            #pragma omp barrier\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Final state is in buffer 0 if N_ITER even, else buffer 1 (since we start with src=0)\n    int final_idx = (N_ITER % 2 == 0) ? 0 : 1;\n\n    // Write output as Body structs to match expected binary format\n    Body *out = (Body *)malloc((size_t)nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n    if (!out) { perror(\"malloc\"); exit(EXIT_FAILURE); }\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        out[i].x = px[final_idx][i];\n        out[i].y = py[final_idx][i];\n        out[i].z = pz[final_idx][i];\n        out[i].vx = vx[final_idx][i];\n        out[i].vy = vy[final_idx][i];\n        out[i].vz = vz[final_idx][i];\n    }\n    if (fwrite(out, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout) != 1) {\n        fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n\n    free(out);\n    for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {\n        free(px[b]); free(py[b]); free(pz[b]);\n        free(vx[b]); free(vy[b]); free(vz[b]);\n    }\n    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n```\n\nThis integrates all three sources: SoA (A), unrolled inner loop (B), double buffering + manual partitioning + explicit barrier (C). We also used contiguous blocks instead of strided for better cache locality, but that's still manual partitioning.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with this file content. Also include hypothesis and transformations list.\n\nTransformations:\n- Structure-of-arrays (SoA) data layout for positions and velocities (from A)\n- OpenMP parallelization over outer body-index loop with static scheduling? Actually we used manual partitioning, not worksharing; but the idea of parallelizing over bodies is from A. We can say \"OpenMP parallelization over outer body-index loop\" without specifying schedule.\n- Unrolled inner j-loop by a factor of 4 with independent sqrt/division computations (from B)\n- Double buffering using alternating arrays (from C)\n- Manual OpenMP thread partitioning instead of worksharing loop (from C)\n- Explicit barrier for synchronization (from C)\n\nWe'll list these.\n\nsources_kept: all three IDs.\n\nNow produce JSON."
